      "content": "The total sum $V$ is the sum of 8 fair six-sided dice, $V = D_1 + D_2 + \\dots + D_8$.\nWe have observed $D_1=3$ and $D_2=4$.\nThe remaining 6 dice ($D_3, \\dots, D_8$) are still hidden.\nThe sum of the observed dice is $S_{obs} = 3 + 4 = 7$.\nThe remaining sum $V_{rem} = D_3 + \\dots + D_8$ is the sum of 6 fair six-sided dice.\n$V = 7 + V_{rem}$.\n\nWe need to calculate $M = E[V | D_1=3, D_2=4] = 7 + E[V_{rem}]$.\nThe expected value of a single fair six-sided die is $(1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 3.5$.\n$E[V_{rem}] = 6 \\times 3.5 = 21$.\n$M = 7 + 21 = 28$.\n\n**Strategy:**\nWe aim to set quotes around $M=28$. Since we are risk-neutral, we should price based on expected value.\n\n**Informed Trader (30%):**\nIf $V > \\text{ask}$, they buy the whole ask size.\nIf $V < \\text{bid}$, they sell the whole bid size.\nIf $\\text{bid} \\le V \\le \\text{ask}$, they do nothing.\n\n**Noise Trader (70%):**\nValuation $u = M + e$, where $e \\sim N(0, 16)$.\nIf $u > M$ (i.e., $e>0$), they want to buy (demand up to $u$).\nIf $u < M$ (i.e., $e<0$), they want to sell (demand down to $u$).\nThey trade at our quoted price if it's within their limit.\n\nSince $M=28$, the noise trader's expected behavior is balanced around 28. We should quote symmetrically around 28 to capture expected value from both types of traders without excessive adverse selection risk.\n\nLet's choose a small spread, say 0.5, and sizes of 5 to maximize potential fills without overcommitting.\n\nBid: $28 - 0.5 = 27.5$\nAsk: $28 + 0.5 = 28.5$\n\nWe set sizes to 5 for both sides.\n\n{\"bid\": 27.5, \"bid_size\": 5, \"ask\": 28.5, \"ask_size\": 5}",
